          "def-stiefel-whitney-classes-from-the-projective-bundle-relation"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "statement": "Let $K\\subset S^3$ be a smooth knot with a marked point $x$ and a preferred nonzero normal vector $v$ at $x$. Choose a small disk $D$ normal to $K$ at $x$, let $L=\\partial D$, and let $\\omega$ be the radius of $D$ from $x$ to a point of $L$. Then $K^\\natural:=K\\cup L$ is the based earring link. Choose disjoint closed tubular neighborhoods $N(K)$ and $N(L)$ of its two components, set $X=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}(N(K)\\cup N(L))$, and truncate the endpoints of $\\omega$ at $\\partial X$ to obtain $[\\omega]\\in H_1(X,\\partial X;\\mathbb Z/2)$.\n\nHere $PU(2)=U(2)/U(1)\\cong SO(3)$; a principal $PU(2)$ bundle $P\\to X$ is equivalently an oriented real 3-plane bundle, and $w_2(P)\\in H^2(X;\\mathbb Z/2)$ denotes its second Stiefel–Whitney class. Poincaré–Lefschetz duality for the oriented compact 3-manifold $X$ with boundary is the isomorphism $PD:H_1(X,\\partial X;\\mathbb Z/2)\\to H^2(X;\\mathbb Z/2)$. Include a bundle $P$ with $w_2(P)=PD([\\omega])$ in the singular bundle data.\n\nIn a local meridional angular coordinate $\\theta$, an $SU(2)$ lift of the singular connection has the standard model $A_0+\\frac14\\begin{pmatrix}i&0\\\\0&-i\\end{pmatrix}d\\theta$, where $A_0$ is smooth in an $O(2)$ reduction. Parallel transport (holonomy) around either meridian is conjugate in $SU(2)$ to $\\operatorname{diag}(i,-i)$; its image has order two in $PU(2)$. Define $I^\\natural(K;\\mathbb Z)$ to be the singular instanton Floer homology $I^\\omega(S^3,K^\\natural;P)$ associated to these data whenever its analytic complex and independence of auxiliary choices have been established. Those analytic and invariance results are separate theorem obligations.\n\nFor the sutured version, let $E_K$ be the compact knot exterior and put two oppositely oriented meridional sutures $\\gamma$ on its boundary torus. Choose a connected oriented annulus $T$, attach $[-1,1]\\times T$ along the annular sutured region, and glue the two resulting boundary surfaces by a diffeomorphism fixing a chosen point from $T$. The result is a closed oriented 3-manifold $\\bar Y$ with a distinguished torus $\\bar R$; the fixed point determines a standard circle meeting $\\bar R$ once. Choose a line bundle $w\\to\\bar Y$ whose first Chern class is Poincaré dual to that circle, so $\\langle c_1(w),[\\bar R]\\rangle$ is odd. Let $I_*(\\bar Y,w;\\mathbb Q)$ denote the closed instanton Floer group with rational coefficients, when its analytic construction is available. For a homology class $z$ of dimension $0$ or $2$ in $\\bar Y$, let $\\mu(z)$ denote the standard instanton Floer operator associated to $z$; the point and surface operators commute and have degrees $4$ and $-2$, respectively. If $y\\in\\bar R$, define the closure group $KHI_{\\bar Y,\\bar R,w}(K;\\mathbb Q)$ by\n$$\nKHI_{\\bar Y,\\bar R,w}(K;\\mathbb Q)\n:=\\{a\\in I_*(\\bar Y,w;\\mathbb Q):\\mu(\\bar R)a=0,\\ \\mu(y)a=2a\\}.\n$$\nThis is the simultaneous eigenspace with eigenvalues $(0,2)$ specified by the instanton closure convention. Independence of the closure is asserted only after the separate sutured-instanton theorem is proved.",
